      "assumptions": "The requirement states notifications are sent to all addresses *except* postal addresses. If no other address exists, the behavior for postal-only accounts is not explicitly defined in KDH-005, but the exception implies postal is excluded. However, NIST 800-63B-4 says postal *shall* be sent if no other form is stored. KDH-005 says 'except postal addresses'. This creates a conflict or a specific interpretation. Let's assume KDH-005 strictly follows the 'except postal' rule for the test, meaning if only postal is present, no notification is sent via the 'all except postal' logic, OR the system handles the 'no other address' case separately. Given the prompt asks to test KDH-005 which says 'except postal', I will test that postal is excluded. If the system is required to send to postal when it's the only one, that would be a different requirement or a conflict. I will assume the test verifies that postal is NOT sent as per the 'except' clause, or that the system handles the edge case. Let's look at the NIST text provided: 'Notifications SHALL be sent to all notification addresses except postal addresses. However, notifications SHALL be sent to postal addresses if no other form of notification address is stored...'. KDH-005 cites NIST 800-63B-4 §4.6. Therefore, KDH-005 implies the full NIST logic. So, if only postal is stored, it SHOULD be sent. But the requirement text says 'except postal addresses'. This is ambiguous. I will write a test for the standard case (multiple addresses) and a boundary case where only postal is present to see if it follows the NIST exception (send to postal) or the literal 'except' (send nothing). Given the citation, it should follow NIST. So, if only postal is present, it should be sent. But the requirement text explicitly says 'except postal'. I will assume the requirement text is the primary source for the test, but the citation implies the full rule. I will create a test for the 'only postal' scenario expecting it to be sent, as per the NIST rule cited, or if the requirement is strictly 'except', then no notification. Let's stick to the explicit text of KDH-005: 'except postal addresses'. If I test 'only postal', and the system sends it, it violates the literal text but follows the citation. If it doesn't send it, it follows the literal text but violates the citation. I will assume the requirement intends to follow the NIST standard fully. Therefore, the 'except' is the general rule, and the 'only postal' is the exception. I will test the general rule in TC-001/002. I will add a test for the 'only postal' case expecting it to be sent, as per the NIST rule referenced.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "no": 1,
          "action": "Trigger an account notification event for the company representative account.",
          "expected": "The system sends a notification to the registered postal address, as it is the only notification address stored."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

